{"causes": ["The ongoing geopolitical rivalry between the Sasanian Persian Empire and the Roman Empire for control over the Caucasus directly necessitated Iberian kings to align with one power or the other.", "The death of pro-Roman King Sauromaces II created a power vacuum that the Sasanian Empire exploited to install a more amenable ruler like Aspacures III.", "The strategic location of Iberia (Kartli) as a buffer state between major empires made its internal politics highly susceptible to external intervention and influence."], "effects": ["Aspacures III's reign solidified Sasanian influence over Eastern Iberia, contributing to a period of increased Persian cultural and political presence in the region.", "The division of Iberia into spheres of influence, formalized around his reign by treaties like Acilisene, significantly constrained the kingdom's sovereignty and future development.", "His rule contributed to the complex historical pattern of fluctuating allegiances for Georgian monarchs, shaping the region's geopolitical identity for centuries."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "sasanian-empire", "sourceName": "Sasanian Empire", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "aspacures-iii-of-iberia", "targetName": "Aspacures III of Iberia", "context": "The Sasanian Empire exerted significant political and military influence over Aspacures III's accession and reign in Iberia (c. 380-390 CE)."}, {"sourceSlug": "aspacures-iii-of-iberia", "sourceName": "Aspacures III of Iberia", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "late-roman-empire", "targetName": "Late Roman Empire", "context": "Aspacures III's reign (c. 380-390 CE) coincided with the period of the Late Roman Empire, which was a major imperial power contending for influence in the Caucasus."}, {"sourceSlug": "aspacures-iii-of-iberia", "sourceName": "Aspacures III of Iberia", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "iberia-caucasus", "targetName": "Iberia (Caucasus)", "context": "Aspacures III ruled as King of Iberia (Kartli), the ancient kingdom located in the Caucasus region, corresponding to modern-day Georgia."}, {"sourceSlug": "treaty-of-acilisene", "sourceName": "Treaty of Acilisene", "verb": "FRAMES", "targetSlug": "aspacures-iii-of-iberia", "targetName": "Aspacures III of Iberia", "context": "The Treaty of Acilisene (387 CE), which divided Iberia, framed the geopolitical realities and limitations of Aspacures III's later reign."}, {"sourceSlug": "aspacures-iii-of-iberia", "sourceName": "Aspacures III of Iberia", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "roman-persian-wars", "targetName": "Roman–Persian Wars", "context": "Aspacures III, as a ruler of a buffer state, was indirectly involved in the broader Roman-Persian Wars of the 4th century CE through shifting allegiances."}], "places": [{"name": "Mtskheta, Georgia", "role": "Capital City"}, {"name": "Caucasus Mountains, Eurasia", "role": "Geopolitical Region"}, {"name": "Ctesiphon, Iraq", "role": "Sasanian Imperial Capital"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Aspacures III of Iberia", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "aspacures-iii-of-iberia"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3554688", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aspacures_III"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 5, "significanceNarrative": "Aspacures III's reign is notable for illustrating the diminishing autonomy of Iberian kings caught between Roman and Sasanian imperial ambitions, contributing to the formal division of Iberia and solidifying Persian influence in its eastern half. His period reflects a broader trend of smaller states adapting to the dictates of larger powers in the late 4th century CE.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-05T11:38:41Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#28739375584", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Aspacures III of Iberia was a economist or social scientist of the Classical era, an age of empires, philosophical inquiry, and foundational civilizations, active in Global. Known in the fields of monarchs & rulers.", "newValue": "Aspacures III of Iberia, also known as Varaz-Bakur II, reigned as King of Iberia (Kartli, modern Georgia) from approximately 380 to 390 CE. A monarch of the Pharnavazid dynasty, his tenure was deeply entangled in the geopolitical machinations of the late Classical era, specifically the intensifying rivalry between the Sasanian Persian Empire and the Roman Empire (later Byzantine) for suzerainty over the strategically vital Caucasus region. Aspacures III's rule represents a period when Iberian sovereignty was increasingly constrained by these two colossal powers, forcing its rulers into a precarious balancing act.

During his reign, Aspacures III largely operated under Sasanian influence, likely having been installed or supported by the Persian court following the death of his predecessor, Sauromaces II, who had maintained pro-Roman leanings. This shift underscored a temporary but significant tilt in Iberian foreign policy towards Persia, impacting religious and cultural developments within the kingdom. His rule coincided with the formalization of the division of Iberia into Roman and Sasanian spheres of influence, a process solidified by the Treaty of Acilisene in 387 CE, which effectively carved up the kingdom into eastern (Persian-dominated) and western (Roman-dominated) zones.

